A||||8||GT Bastard||Effects of oil wieght on performance?||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-25-2001||12:32 AM||||Does anybody have some kindof formula to calculate what the weight of the oil has on performance?  Say you're going from 5w30 to 20w50 oil.  Or using straight sae50 weight vs. 20w50.  Any info?||||reg||4
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||04-25-2001||01:57 AM|||| I stand corrected..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000002||Oreif||04-25-2001||02:15 AM||||5W-30 in an engine that has high mileage or is run hard is not a good idea. The oil is too thin. It is OK for winter use due to extreme temps and most folks don't "hod rod" the car in the extreme cold. I use 10W-40 normally. If you tend to stay in the upper RPM range 20W-50 would be better. For turbo'd engines 10W-40 is best, For overhead cam/multi-valve heads or newer cars 5W-30 is better. Newer engines have tighter tolerences and the thinner oil is needed. For high mileage engines that are driven hard, 20W-50. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||04-25-2001||01:37 PM||||I understand that, but does anybody have any info about the effects of heavier oil on performance?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spektyr||04-25-2001||03:44 PM||||Well, I'm coming from a pure physics side here... no experience to back it up.<P>Thicker oil is harder to pump and is slightly less 'slippery', so it will use more engine hp to run the oil pump.  However, it will 'fill up' the bigger gaps in an older motor.<P>Thinner oil runs through the pump easier, saving hps, but in an older motor, or a particularly hot running motor, it fails to lubricate enough and faces more thermal break down.<P>Like I said though, this is based on a scientific approach, I'll leave it to others to check my work for accuracy.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000005||KRMFiero||04-25-2001||05:05 PM||||My duke seemed to run better/quicker with 20w-50 senthetic blend over the old no name 10w-30        not sure though, it just said High Performance on the oil container so i bought a case  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and was like $3.** a quart heh||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-25-2001||07:27 PM||||There probably is some formula somplace to predict this..... the simple fact is that you have to try diferant grades/brands and see what works best in your specific setup. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||04-25-2001||07:32 PM||||A way to tell is the energy conservation seal on the label for the oil container.  Lighter weight oils have a amarking showing they conserivve fuel and heavy wts and straight wt oils do not have this.  If it conserves fuel it will also give a bit more power. Tink of how much power you would lose with a crankcase full of STP. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||04-30-2001||10:23 AM||||You guys will probably think I'm nuts, but I use Mobil 1 0W30 year round. <P>And I don't use any more oil than normal. Mileage is probably 5-10% better and the car seems quicker.<P>This oil is actually rated for higher temps than the 5W30 or the 15W50 that Mobil 1 also comes in.||||reg||1
